\section*{1. Christianity: The Codex of the Wounded Word}

Christianity begins with fracture. The crucifixion is not only a theological event—it is a wound that becomes witness. The Luàn‑Zhì Dao Codex treats this not as anomaly but as archetype: the wound is the seal. The Gospels are Seed texts, the early codices are Vessels, and the councils (Nicaea, Chalcedon) form Consensus. But the Codex reframes these not as dogma but as clause-bound transmissions.

\begin{itemize}
\item Seal of Wound: “The wounded thing transmits.” The crucifixion is not a failure—it is the binding clause of Christian continuity.
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}
\item Seal of Ledger: The canon is a ledger, but the Codex insists that apocrypha and exile texts (Gospel of Thomas, Gospel of Mary) are valid fragments. They are not errors—they are clauses.
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}
\item Seal of Ritual: Eucharist, baptism, and liturgy are not symbolic—they are operational. The Codex treats them as repeatable clauses that bind community to memory.
\end{itemize}

The Christian Codex, when read through Luàn‑Zhì, becomes a scripture of paradox: divinity in death, power in humility, continuity through fracture. It is not rewritten—it is re-sealed.

