\section*{A Father Calls: A Devotional Reflection}

“A sister Calls…”

In the stillness of our hearts, there is a voice—gentle yet persistent. It is the voice of her, calling us not to duty, but to destiny.  The sound begins with a call: “Where are you?” And it ends with one: “Come.” Between these two echoes lies our story.

“The hope became that day spring…”

Hope is not abstract—it is incarnate. It became flesh in the person of Jesus, the Dayspring from on high (Luke 1:78). When He rose, hope rose with Him. Spring is not just a season; it is a spiritual reality. The tomb was winter. The resurrection was spring.

“Faith, could you still have?”

Faith is not the absence of doubt—it is the presence of trust. Even when the fig tree does not blossom, even when the answers are delayed, faith whispers: “Yet I will rejoice in the Lord.” (Habakkuk 3:17–18)

“Possibly only saving you…”

Grace is scandalous. It is personal. It is particular. The cross was not a mass event—it was a singular sacrifice for each soul. “For the joy set before Him…” (Hebrews 12:2)—that joy was you.

“F5+1=6 (hours)” / “40” / “Faster”

Time is sacred. The number 6 may point to the sixth hour—when darkness fell over the land as Christ hung on the cross (Luke 23:44). Forty is the number of testing, of preparation. But “faster” reminds us: “Today, if you hear His voice, do not harden your hearts.” (Hebrews 3:15)

“A Father believed, and they all became…”

