\section*{Abstract}

This dissertation advances the Luàn‑Zhì Dao Codex as a new genre of scripture that fuses Daoist aphoristic cadence with a sovereign, clause‑bound system of ritual law. Where the Dao De Jing privileges paradox as description, the Codex codifies paradox as enforceable method. Its central claim is that chaos (Luàn) and will (Zhì) are not opposites but interdependent forces, bound through knots, cuts, reversals, and breaths. These ritualized acts form the foundation of a scripture that is simultaneously philosophical, legal, and liturgical.

The Codex situates itself within a planetary web of traditions: Daoist notions of reversal and effortless flow, Shintō reverence for shrine and purification, Buddhist interdependence and emptiness, Vedic concepts of ṛta (cosmic order) and yajña (sacrificial act), shamanic initiatory wounds and drum‑flight, and indigenous understandings of land and ancestor as covenant. These parallels are not treated as external influences but as root‑threads of a single woven law, indistinguishable in their expression.

Methodologically, the dissertation employs “clause‑bound hermeneutics,” a mode of reading in which each aphorism is treated as both poetic utterance and binding clause. The Codex insists that what is not sealed, witnessed, or wounded cannot transmit. This approach reframes scripture as operational law: doctrine is not merely believed but enacted, archived, and passed forward.

The contribution of the Luàn‑Zhì Dao Codex is threefold. First, it proposes paradox as codified law rather than rhetorical flourish. Second, it reframes scripture as ritual engine: a system designed to survive collapse, adapt through molt, and transmit across generations. Third, it demonstrates how global traditions can be interwoven into a planetary web without loss of sovereignty or descent into syncretism. The Codex is not a hybrid but a root‑text, speaking in one voice that is at once Daoist, Shintō, Buddhist, Vedic, shamanic, and indigenous.

Ultimately, the Codex is a scripture for the broken and imperfect, framed in blinding, unyielding love, community, and YIN. It declares the end of forgetting and the dawn of continuity.

