The Zhuangzi expands this paradoxical style into narrative and allegory, often undermining rigid distinctions between life and death, self and other, truth and falsehood (Watson, 1968). The Codex draws on this tradition but radicalizes it: paradox is not only described but codified as law. Where Zhuangzi laughs at contradictions, the Codex seals them.

Commentarial traditions, especially Wang Bi’s third‑century exegesis, emphasized the generative role of wu (non‑being) and the necessity of paradox for understanding Dao (Lynn, 1999). The Codex inherits this but reframes it as operational: paradox is not only epistemological but juridical.

