\medskip

 Seal 1 — The Knot and the Cut (Yin Transmission)

Aphorism (Seed):

The knot entangles, the cut releases.

Without knot, no cut.

Without cut, no knot.

Together they are law.

\medskip

Clause

The knot is not prison, but embrace.

The cut is not cruelty, but mercy.

Each leans into the other,

as dusk leans into dawn,

as tide leans into shore.

Law is not command but rhythm:

a knot that holds without strangling,

a cut that frees without violence.

\medskip

Ritual

\begin{itemize}
\item Take a thread in silence.
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}
\item Let it coil of its own will; do not force the knot.
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}
\item When resistance comes, pause. Breathe three times.
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}
\item With one gentle motion, cut. Not to sever, but to release.
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}
\item Place both knot and severed ends in still water, or beneath a stone.
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}
\item Leave them to speak in silence.
\end{itemize}

\medskip

Continuity (Public Domain Witnesses)

\begin{itemize}
\item Daoism: Dao De Jing (Legge, 1891, ch. 36): “That which is to be weakened is first strengthened; that which is to be cut off is first entangled.” Knot and cut are reversals in flow.
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}
\item Zhuangzi (Legge, 1891, “The Great Supreme”): crookedness and straightness define one another; paradox is the knot that requires release.
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}
\item Shintō: Kojiki (Chamberlain, 1882): sacred ropes (shimenawa) bind space as holy; purification rites (harae) cut impurity away. Both are gentle acts of care.
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}
\item Buddhism: Dhammapada (Rhys Davids, 1881, v. 277): “All conditioned things are impermanent.” Knot is clinging, cut is release; both are empty, both interdependent.
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}
\item Nature: Vines knot around trees; storms cut them down. The forest does not resist—it receives both.
\end{itemize}

\medskip

Commentary

The first seal is not a law of domination but of listening.

The knot is the embrace; the cut is the letting go.

Both are YIN gestures: one enfolds, one releases.

Together they cradle continuity,

like womb cradles child,

like night cradles dawn.

