\section*{2. Shintō Resonances}

Shintō, Japan’s indigenous tradition, emphasizes ritual purity, shrine presence, and reverence for kami (spirits). The Kojiki (712 CE) and Nihon Shoki (720 CE) provide mythic foundations, while the Engishiki (927 CE) preserves norito prayers and ritual codes (Aston, 1896; Chamberlain, 1882).

The Codex resonates with Shintō in its treatment of place as witness. Just as shrines are not symbolic but loci of divine presence, the Codex treats groves, rivers, and mountains as planetary seals. The norito prayers, with their formulaic, clause‑bound structure, anticipate the Codex’s aphoristic clauses. Purification (harae) parallels the Codex’s “cut” seal: a ritual act that removes impurity and restores continuity.

Scholars such as John Breen and Mark Teeuwen (A New History of Shinto, 2010) emphasize Shintō’s adaptability and ritual centrality. The Codex aligns with this adaptability, treating ritual not as static but as a living engine of continuity.

