Buddhism contributes the doctrines of interdependence (pratītyasamutpāda) and emptiness (śūnyatā). The Pali Canon (Rhys Davids, 1899) preserves early teachings on dependent origination: all phenomena arise in relation, none stand alone. Nāgārjuna’s Mūlamadhyamakakārikā (2nd century CE) radicalizes this into emptiness: all things are empty of inherent existence because they are dependently arisen (Garfield, 1995).

The Codex resonates here by treating exile, fragment, and wound not as failures but as conditions of continuity. Just as emptiness is not nihilism but interdependence, the Codex’s paradox is not contradiction but binding. Buddhist chanting traditions, from Pali gāthās to Zen sutra recitations, exemplify the Codex’s “memory” seal: oral repetition as transmission.

Mahāyāna sutras such as the Lotus Sutra emphasize the universality of salvation and the adaptability of teaching (Kern, 1884). The Codex echoes this universality: every fragment, every exile, every wound is included.

