Jacques Derrida’s Force of Law (1990) explores the paradox of justice: law requires interpretation, which destabilizes its authority. Giorgio Agamben’s Homo Sacer (1998) examines the state of exception, where law includes by excluding. Gilles Deleuze’s Logic of Sense (1969) treats paradox as generative.

The Codex aligns with these but reframes them. Paradox is not destabilization but law itself. Exile and fragment are not exceptions but clauses. The Codex codifies what philosophy has described.

