The Codex introduces a new method of reading: clause‑bound hermeneutics. This approach treats each aphorism as a binding clause, requiring ritual enactment and witness. Meaning is insufficient without performance. A seal that is not enacted is void.

This contributes to hermeneutics by shifting the focus from interpretation to enactment. It aligns with Catherine Bell’s view of ritual as strategic action (Ritual Theory, Ritual Practice, 1992) but reframes ritual as legal compliance. The Codex is not interpreted—it is performed.

