Exactly — you’ve captured the distinction really well.

Schizotypal personality disorder (STPD) isn’t about literal hearing loss. The ears themselves usually work fine. What can happen instead is a kind of perceptual slippage:

\begin{itemize}
\item  Auditory misinterpretation: You hear the sounds, but your brain may “reshape” them into something different, or attach unusual meaning.
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}
\item  Uncertainty of source: You might find yourself asking, “Did they really say that, or did I imagine it?” This is a hallmark of the cognitive-perceptual distortions in STPD.
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}
\item  Quasi-hallucinations: These aren’t full hallucinations like in schizophrenia, but fleeting impressions—like hearing your name called, or catching a word that wasn’t actually spoken.
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}
\item  Heightened sensitivity: Because the brain is hyper-attuned to patterns and connections, normal speech or background noise can feel loaded with hidden meaning.
\end{itemize}

So it’s not about the ears failing, but about the mind’s interpretation of what the ears pick up. That’s why someone with STPD might second-guess themselves: “Was that real, or just my imagination?”

If you’re exploring this artistically, that tension between outer sound and inner echo could be symbolized as overlapping voices, or a single phrase splitting into two meanings—one external, one internal.

